      "current_source": {
        "path": "items/lem-qft-photon-covariance-spectrum.md",
        "sha256": "4030ae1de8216c21246b062f14dc25327d6ebea19ac4f188b989972ce77291c3"
      },
      "reviewed_source_excerpts": [
        "Assume Countable Choice. On the future null cone write $p=(\\omega,\\mathbf k)$, $\\omega=|\\mathbf k|$, $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, and use the measure $d\\nu=d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\omega)$. The fiber $p^\\perp/\\mathbb Cp$ has the positive quotient form of [[lem-qft-photon-null-quotient]]. Give the proper orthochronous Lorentz group its matrix topology: the subspace topology inherited from $\\mathbb R^{16}$ with Euclidean matrix norm $\\|A\\|_{\\rm mat}=(\\sum_{i,j=0}^3|A_{ij}|^2)^{1/2}$. For each proper orthochronous Lorentz transformation $\\Lambda$, the action\n$$[s(p)]\\longmapsto[\\Lambda s(\\Lambda^{-1}p)]$$\nis a unitary representation on the square-integrable quotient sections. It is strongly continuous for this topology, meaning $\\|U_\\Lambda s-U_{\\Lambda_0}s\\|\\to0$ for every section $s$ as $\\|\\Lambda-\\Lambda_0\\|_{\\rm mat}\\to0$. Its bosonic Fock lift is strongly continuous for the same topology. Under the unitary identification with $\\mathcal H_T$ given by\n$T_p[\\epsilon]:=\\boldsymbol\\epsilon-(\\mathbf k/\\omega)\\epsilon^0$\nand $u(\\mathbf k)=T_p[s(p)]/\\sqrt{2\\omega}$, as in [[lem-qft-photon-null-quotient]], the free translation representation for $a=(a^0,\\mathbf a)\\in\\mathbb R^4$ has phase $e^{i(\\omega a^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf a)}$. Its bosonic Fock lift has the joint PVM $\\mathsf E$ whose $n$-particle sector acts by multiplication by $\\mathbf1_{\\{(\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|,-\\sum_{r=1}^n\\mathbf k_r)\\in B\\}}$ for Borel $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$; on the vacuum it acts by $\\mathbf1_B(0)$. Define its joint spectrum to be\n$$\\operatorname{supp}(\\mathsf E):=\\{q\\in\\mathbb R^4:\\mathsf E(U)\\ne0\\text{ for every open neighborhood }U\\text{ of }q\\}.$$\nThen this joint spectrum is the closed future cone\n$$\\overline V_+=\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\},$$\nwith the vacuum at $(0,\\mathbf0)$. In the product realization of the sectors in [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]], on sector $n$ the energy operator multiplies by $\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|$ and has the exact maximal domain\n$$D(H_E)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{(\\mathbb R^3)^n}\\left(\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|\\right)^2\\|\\Psi_n(\\mathbf k_1,\\ldots,\\mathbf k_n)\\|^2\\,d^3\\mathbf k_1\\cdots d^3\\mathbf k_n<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nwith the vacuum component unrestricted; here $\\Psi_n$ is the symmetric transverse wavefunction in the product realization and the norm is the product-fiber Hilbert norm. Each momentum operator $P_j$ multiplies by $-\\sum_{r=1}^n k_{r,j}$ and has maximal domain\n$$D(P_j)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{(\\mathbb R^3)^n}\\left|\\sum_{r=1}^n k_{r,j}\\right|^2\\|\\Psi_n(\\mathbf k_1,\\ldots,\\mathbf k_n)\\|^2\\,d^3\\mathbf k_1\\cdots d^3\\mathbf k_n<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nagain with unrestricted vacuum component. These are mathematical free-field representation statements.",
        "Matrix multiplication is continuous, and the Lorentz equation gives $\\Lambda^{-1}=\\eta\\Lambda^T\\eta$, so inversion is continuous in the stated matrix topology. For $v\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^3;\\mathbb C^3)$ with support in $B_R$, $R\\ge1$, take the four-component representative $s_v(p)=(0,\\sqrt{2\\omega}\\,P_T(\\mathbf k)v(\\mathbf k))$; under the stated unitary identification its class corresponds to $u=P_Tv\\in\\mathcal H_T$. Put $q=\\Lambda^{-1}p$ and $a_\\Lambda(\\mathbf k)=T_p\\Lambda(0,\\sqrt{2q^0}\\,P_T(\\mathbf q)v(\\mathbf q))$, the transverse chart representative of the transformed quotient section. If $\\Lambda$ is sufficiently close to $I$, uniformly for future null $p$ one has $\\omega/2\\le q^0=|\\mathbf q|\\le2\\omega$ and $\\|\\Lambda\\|_{\\rm op}\\le2$: use $\\|p\\|_2=\\sqrt2\\omega$, the displayed inverse formula and the Euclidean Cauchy–Schwarz bound for a matrix acting on a vector. Thus $a_\\Lambda$ vanishes outside $B_{2R}$, and $\\|T_p\\|_{\\rm op}\\le\\sqrt2$, $\\|P_T\\|_{\\rm op}\\le1$ give $|a_\\Lambda(\\mathbf k)|\\le C M\\sqrt\\omega$, where $M=\\sup|v|$ and $C$ is independent of sufficiently small $\\Lambda-I$. Consequently $|a_\\Lambda-a_I|$ is bounded by a fixed constant $C_R M$ on the common support. Its squared $L^2(d\\nu)$ contribution on $B_\\delta$ is at most $C_R^2M^2\\nu(B_\\delta)=C_R^2M^2\\pi\\delta^2$ by [F6, F10]. On the annulus $\\delta\\le|\\mathbf k|\\le2R$, extend the explicit formula to matrices $A$ in a sufficiently small closed Euclidean matrix box about $I$ by putting $q=\\eta A^T\\eta p$. On that box $q^0$ and $|\\mathbf q|$ remain positive, bounded away from zero on the annulus; the formula using $P_T(\\mathbf q)=I-(\\mathbf q/|\\mathbf q|)(\\mathbf q/|\\mathbf q|)^T$ is therefore continuous. The box times annulus is closed bounded in $\\mathbb R^{19}$, hence compact, and [F11] gives uniform continuity of this formula there. Restricting back to Lorentz matrices yields $a_\\Lambda\\to a_I$ uniformly on the annulus, which has finite $\\nu$-measure by [F6]. First choose $\\delta$, then $\\Lambda-I$, to obtain $\\|a_\\Lambda-a_I\\|_{L^2(d\\nu)}\\to0$; this is exactly the quotient-section norm. No fiber value at the omitted apex is used. These sections are dense by [F4, F7]: approximate any $u\\in\\mathcal H_T$ by smooth compactly supported $v$ and project by $P_T$. Unitarity from step 2.1 gives $\\|U_\\Lambda s-s\\|\\le2\\|s-s_v\\|+\\|U_\\Lambda s_v-s_v\\|$, extending continuity to every section. At any $\\Lambda_0$, the representation identity reduces continuity to $U_{\\Lambda_0^{-1}\\Lambda}\\to I$, and $\\Lambda_0^{-1}\\Lambda\\to I$ in the matrix topology. For an elementary $n$-tensor, telescoping and unitarity give $\\|(U_\\Lambda^{\\otimes n}-I)(h_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes h_n)\\|\\le\\sum_{j=1}^n\\|(U_\\Lambda-I)h_j\\|\\prod_{l\\ne j}\\|h_l\\|\\to0$. Finite linear combinations are dense in the Hilbert tensor product; the symmetric projection is a contraction commuting with the tensor action, so continuity holds on each symmetric sector. The vacuum is fixed. Truncating an arbitrary Fock vector to finitely many sectors leaves an error bounded by twice its tail norm under these unitary maps; letting that tail tend to zero proves strong continuity on all Fock space."
      ]
